The array can be split into two parts. Each part gets its own sum, and the two results are added together. This is mainly a different way to organize the work, since every element is still visited once.

```go
func arraySum(nums []int) int {
    var sumRange func(int, int) int

    sumRange = func(left int, right int) int {
        if left >= right {
            return 0
        }
        if right-left == 1 {
            return nums[left]
        }

        middle := left + (right-left)/2
        return sumRange(left, middle) + sumRange(middle, right)
    }

    return sumRange(0, len(nums))
}
```

The base case for an empty range returns zero, so an empty slice is safe. The slice is not modified.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive split calls.

A direct loop is enough here. Start with zero and add each value as the array is scanned once. It also works when the array has only one value.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int arraySum(vector<int>& nums){
        int total = 0;
        for (int value : nums) {
            total += value;
        }
        return total;
    }
};
```

For example, with `[4, -1, 6]`, the running total becomes `4`, then `3`, then `9`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The input array is not changed.

A running total is the simplest manual implementation. The code stores the total in a `long` while adding values, then returns the required `int` result from the platform method.

```java
class Solution {
    public int arraySum(int[] nums) {
        long total = 0L;
        for (int value : nums) {
            total += value;
        }
        return (int) total;
    }
}
```

For `[3, 0, -2, 7]`, the total changes to `3`, `3`, `1`, and `8`. Using `long` for the calculation gives more room before the final required conversion to `int`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This reads every item once and leaves the input array as it was.

This version adds the first value and then asks a helper to add the rest. The helper stops when its index reaches the array length. It is a little less practical than a loop because recursion uses stack space, but the steps are easy to follow.

```java
class Solution {
    public int arraySum(int[] nums) {
        return addFrom(nums, 0);
    }

    private int addFrom(int[] nums, int index) {
        if (index == nums.length) {
            return 0;
        }
        return nums[index] + addFrom(nums, index + 1);
    }
}
```

A dry run for `[2, 5, 1]` is `2 + add(1)`, then `5 + add(2)`, and finally `1 + add(3)`. The last call returns `0`, so the result is `8`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive calls.

The array is only read. Very large arrays may make the recursive version less suitable because of stack limits.

Python already has a built-in function for adding all values in a list. Calling `sum` keeps the method short, and it returns `0` for an empty list, which is the expected starting total.

```python
class Solution:
    def arraySum(self, nums):
        return sum(nums)
```

An empty input is handled without any special code outside the method. The list itself stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, aside from the small value used internally by `sum`.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{	
	public:
		int arraySum(vector<int>& nums){
            if(nums.empty()) return 0;
			int a = nums.back();
            nums.pop_back();
            return a + arraySum(nums);
        }
};
```

## Intuition
Dont calculate len() everytime in the recursion, if we know the max index value upfront. 

## Approach
calculate n = len(nums) -1 and store the max index value possibl, as soon as you cross that return 0 within the recursive function

